<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Radon in Moose Jaw gets less attention than in Regina, partly because Moose Jaw wasn't broken out in the 2024 Cross-Canada survey. The most local figure is older: in Health Canada's 2012 survey, 12.7% of the 118 homes tested in the Five Hills health region, around Moose Jaw, were above 200 Bq/m3. That unit is becquerels per cubic metre, a measure of how much radioactivity is in the air.</p><p>A regional figure from homes that happened to be tested tells you nothing about your own house, and Health Canada says there are no areas of Canada that are radon-free. The honest first step is a <a href="/radon-testing/">long-term test</a> in the lowest part of the home you live in. It's slow, it's cheap, and it answers the question. If you want help planning it, or a result has already come back high, call (204) 555-0173.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Do in Moose Jaw</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">Services for Moose Jaw Properties</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Long-term radon testing</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A test of at least 91 days through the heating season, placed in the lowest lived-in level. In most older Moose Jaw houses that means the basement bedroom, rec room or suite.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Radon mitigation</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Sub-slab depressurization: a pipe through the basement slab, a fan, and discharge outside. A C-NRPP certified mitigation professional quotes it after seeing the house, often travelling in from Regina.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Radon and a home sale</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Radon came up in the inspection or the conditions on a Moose Jaw purchase? A radon professional can explain what a short test can and can't tell you before closing; the paperwork is a question for your realtor or lawyer.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Fan replacement and system checks</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Fan gone loud, gone quiet, or the manometer reading level? Health Canada's national estimate for a replacement fan is $200 to $300, and a professional can check the rest of the system while they're there.</p>
      </div>
</div>
<style>
