</style>
  </div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What the numbers say about Moose Jaw</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Health Canada calls radon the number one cause of lung cancer in non-smokers and estimates it's behind about 16% of lung cancers, more than 3,000 deaths a year in Canada. For smokers it's the second leading cause. That's the serious part, and it's the reason to test. It isn't a reason to panic.</p><p>The Five Hills health region figure above is from 2012, and only 0.8% of those homes were above 600 Bq/m3. Moose Jaw isn't in the 2024 survey's city tables at all. Of the Regina homes tested in 2024, 46.6% were at or above 200 Bq/m3, but that's a different city and the two surveys were run differently, so read it as context for the area, not a forecast for your street or a trend.</p><p>200 Bq/m3 is Health Canada's guideline for the average yearly level in the part of the home you use 4 or more hours a day. It's a guideline, not a law: Saskatchewan doesn't require homeowners to test or fix. Above 200, Health Canada recommends corrective action within a year, and below it, there's no level considered risk free. For anyone comparing with US figures, 200 Bq/m3 is about 5.4 pCi/L.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Testing an older Moose Jaw house</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Much of Moose Jaw's older housing near downtown has full basements, and plenty of those are now bedrooms, rec rooms or basement suites. If someone spends 4 or more hours a day down there, that's where the long-term test goes. A crawl space, an unfinished basement, or the furnace or laundry room doesn't count as the normal occupancy area.</p><p>Health Canada wants at least 3 months, with 91 days as the minimum in the heating season, and 3 to 12 months is better. Put the detector in the breathing zone, at least 30 cm off the floor and 30 cm from walls, and away from sumps, vents, windows and heat. One Canadian supplier sells long-term kits for $70 before tax.</p><p>The Moose Jaw Public Library lends radon detectors from October 1 to April 1, free with a library card. Treat a borrowed monitor as a screening check: a quick first look, not a replacement for the long-term test. If you've recently redone windows, insulation or air sealing in an older house, Health Canada says to test again afterwards.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Fixing a high reading, and what it costs</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>The usual fix is <a href="/radon-mitigation/">sub-slab depressurization</a>: a pipe through the basement floor, a fan, and a discharge outside. Health Canada recommends a C-NRPP certified mitigation professional. Certification is voluntary, since no province regulates radon professionals, so look the person up in C-NRPP's online directory. Any sump pit gets sealed, and you test again after the install.</p><p>We found no advertised mitigation price for Moose Jaw. The nearest is a <a href="/radon-regina/">Regina installer</a> quoting $2,500 to $3,500, with crawl spaces or multiple suction points costing more, and Winnipeg installers advertise systems from about $1,500 to $3,000. Your number comes from the professional who sees your house; the <a href="/radon-mitigation-cost/">mitigation cost page</a> has the rest.</p><p>The Canadian Lung Association's Lungs Matter program offers up to $1,500 to low- or moderate-income households, but you need a 90-plus day C-NRPP test above 200 and a C-NRPP quote before the work starts, and funding is limited. Lung Saskatchewan has offered reimbursement in the past, so check its site. Lung Saskatchewan also says radon mitigation counts as an eligible expense under the Saskatchewan Home Renovation Tax Credit; confirm with CRA.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF2F7;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Is radon a problem in Moose Jaw?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">It can be, and only a test will tell you about your house. In Health Canada's 2012 survey, 12.7% of tested homes in the Five Hills health region around Moose Jaw were above 200 Bq/m3, and the city isn't in the 2024 survey tables. Health Canada says no part of Canada is radon-free, so a long-term basement test is the sensible start.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I borrow a radon detector from the Moose Jaw library?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Yes. The Moose Jaw Public Library lends radon detectors from October 1 to April 1, free with a library card. They're good for screening, but Health Canada's standard is a long-term test of at least 91 days, so follow up with one before deciding on mitigation.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Will a radon professional come out to Moose Jaw from Regina?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Radon professionals who serve Moose Jaw usually come from Regina, 45 minutes east. Whoever quotes the job, check them in C-NRPP's online directory first, because the Lungs Matter program needs a C-NRPP quote. Call (204) 555-0173 and we'll put you in touch with an independent professional who covers the city.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">I'm buying an older house in Moose Jaw. Do I have to test for radon?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">No Saskatchewan rule requires a radon test or radon disclosure at sale. Sellers in Saskatchewan must disclose known latent defects, and radon conditions or holdbacks are something to settle with your realtor or lawyer. Our <a href="/radon-testing-home-sale/">home sale page</a> covers the testing side.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
